WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.373
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.673 --> 00:00:09.362
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.662 --> 00:00:16.981
Unit twenty-five: Startups in Southeast Asia. Today you will describe companies concisely with reduced relative clauses.

4
00:00:17.281 --> 00:00:20.201
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:24.167
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:26.018
Startup.

7
00:00:27.017 --> 00:00:30.909
Startup means a newly established business aiming for rapid growth.

8
00:00:31.517 --> 00:00:34.445
The startup now employs sixty people in Phnom Penh.

9
00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.726
Your turn. Say: Startup.

10
00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:41.591
Startup.

11
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:43.142
Founder.

12
00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:47.125
Founder means a person who establishes a company.

13
00:00:47.717 --> 00:00:50.366
Both founders studied computer science abroad.

14
00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.161
Your turn. Say: Founder.

15
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:56.804
Founder.

16
00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:58.644
Investor.

17
00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:03.345
Investor means a person or firm that puts money into a business.

18
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:06.414
A Malaysian investor led the funding round.

19
00:01:07.017 --> 00:01:09.715
Your turn. Say: Investor.

20
00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:13.545
Investor.

21
00:01:14.283 --> 00:01:15.226
Scale.

22
00:01:16.217 --> 00:01:18.948
Scale means to grow a business significantly.

23
00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:21.991
They plan to scale into Laos next year.

24
00:01:22.583 --> 00:01:24.922
Your turn. Say: Scale.

25
00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:28.698
Scale.

26
00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:30.312
Market.

27
00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:34.187
Market means the potential customers for a product.

28
00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:37.874
The Cambodian market is small but fast-growing.

29
00:01:38.483 --> 00:01:41.031
Your turn. Say: Market.

30
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:44.705
Market.

31
00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:46.474
Innovation.

32
00:01:47.483 --> 00:01:50.447
Innovation means a new method, idea or product.

33
00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:54.466
Bakong was a regional innovation in central bank payments.

34
00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:57.354
Your turn. Say: Innovation.

35
00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.294
Innovation.

36
00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:02.877
Mobile.

37
00:02:03.883 --> 00:02:06.208
Mobile means relating to mobile phones.

38
00:02:06.817 --> 00:02:09.791
Mobile banking reached villages that never had a branch.

39
00:02:10.383 --> 00:02:12.885
Your turn. Say: Mobile.

40
00:02:15.733 --> 00:02:16.583
Mobile.

41
00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:18.374
Ecosystem.

42
00:02:19.383 --> 00:02:23.786
Ecosystem means the network of people and organisations that support startups.

43
00:02:24.383 --> 00:02:28.461
The startup ecosystem now includes incubators and angel investors.

44
00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.946
Your turn. Say: Ecosystem.

45
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.080
Ecosystem.

46
00:02:36.867 --> 00:02:40.111
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:46.261
Visal, people say Cambodia's market is too small for startups. Do you agree?

48
00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:56.307
Not entirely. Startups focusing on mobile payments have grown fast, because phones reached people before banks did.

49
00:02:57.417 --> 00:03:00.798
But can they compete with giants from Singapore and Indonesia?

50
00:03:01.883 --> 00:03:11.896
Some can. Companies built for local needs often understand customers better than foreign firms trying to copy their home model.

51
00:03:12.983 --> 00:03:15.273
What does the ecosystem still lack?

52
00:03:16.383 --> 00:03:27.209
Experienced investors and mentors. Founders backed by regional funds have scaled much faster than those relying only on savings.

53
00:03:28.367 --> 00:03:30.262
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:31.117 --> 00:03:34.254
Grammar focus: Reduced relative clauses.

55
00:03:34.554 --> 00:03:35.683
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:36.383 --> 00:03:39.776
Startups focusing on mobile payments have grown fast.

57
00:03:40.683 --> 00:03:43.774
Companies built for local needs understand customers better.

58
00:03:44.683 --> 00:03:47.890
Founders backed by regional funds have scaled faster.

59
00:03:48.833 --> 00:04:02.515
Reduce 'who/that + active verb' to an -ing form (startups that focus → startups focusing) and 'who/that + be + past participle' to the participle alone (firms that are built → firms built). The main verb must still agree with the noun.

60
00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:07.772
Be careful! Don't say: Startups focused on payments has grown.

61
00:04:09.733 --> 00:04:12.952
Say: Startups focused on payments have grown.

62
00:04:13.252 --> 00:04:14.752
Plural subject: have.

63
00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:18.335
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:20.657
Leapfrogging the Region.

65
00:04:20.957 --> 00:04:28.018
Southeast Asia has become one of the world's most dynamic startup regions, home to more than ten companies valued above a billion dollars.

66
00:04:29.033 --> 00:04:38.127
Much of this growth comes from businesses solving problems created by limited infrastructure, such as unbanked populations and congested cities.

67
00:04:39.133 --> 00:04:46.450
In Cambodia, firms offering mobile wallets, food delivery and online education have attracted growing investment.

68
00:04:47.467 --> 00:04:54.470
Nevertheless, startups based in small markets often need to expand across borders quickly in order to reach profitable scale.

69
00:04:55.467 --> 00:05:02.656
Governments supporting incubators, digital skills and clear regulation are likely to see their ecosystems mature fastest.

70
00:05:03.767 --> 00:05:07.501
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:11.824
Students blank in Phnom Penh often use ride-hailing apps.

72
00:05:12.124 --> 00:05:15.185
A, live. B, living. C, lived.

73
00:05:18.733 --> 00:05:20.698
The answer is B: living.

74
00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:24.546
The network that supports startups is the blank.

75
00:05:24.846 --> 00:05:28.592
A, ecosystem. B, founder. C, market.

76
00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:34.435
The answer is A: ecosystem.

77
00:05:35.583 --> 00:05:38.163
To grow a business significantly is to blank.

78
00:05:38.463 --> 00:05:41.815
A, invest. B, found. C, scale.

79
00:05:45.367 --> 00:05:47.180
The answer is C: scale.

80
00:05:48.317 --> 00:05:50.142
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:50.442 --> 00:05:57.857
A, Apps designed for farmers has spread.. B, Apps designed for farmers have spread.. C, Apps designing for farmers have spread..

82
00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.770
The answer is B: Apps designed for farmers have spread..

83
00:06:05.917 --> 00:06:09.751
According to the reading, startups in small markets often need to…

84
00:06:10.051 --> 00:06:15.562
A, expand across borders quickly. B, avoid investors. C, stay very small.

85
00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.411
The answer is A: expand across borders quickly.

86
00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.728
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:27.583 --> 00:06:30.012
What problems could a Cambodian startup solve?

88
00:06:46.817 --> 00:06:48.317
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:48.617 --> 00:07:26.943
A Cambodian startup could solve many everyday problems. For example, an app connecting farmers directly with restaurants could reduce waste and increase farmers' income. Another opportunity is affordable online tutoring for students living in rural provinces, where qualified teachers are scarce. Healthcare is also promising: a service offering simple telemedicine consultations could save patients long journeys. In my view, the best startups begin with a real local frustration rather than copying a foreign idea.

90
00:07:27.243 --> 00:07:28.952
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:34.511
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and reduced relative clauses.

92
00:07:34.811 --> 00:07:40.621
Today's value is innovation. Try new ideas boldly, and learn from every attempt.

93
00:07:40.921 --> 00:07:44.003
See you in Unit twenty-six. Goodbye!

94
00:07:44.303 --> 00:07:47.211
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

